  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "On a shelf, there are $12$ action movie DVDs and $8$ cartoon DVDs. A man randomly selects $3$ DVDs from the shelf. What is the probability that he has chosen $2$ action movie DVDs and $1$ cartoon DVD? Round your answer to the nearest percent.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to pick $3$ DVDs out of $20,$ and $n$ is the number of ways to choose exactly $2$ action movie DVDs and $1$ cartoon DVD. \n\nSince the order in which he picks the DVDs does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $3$ DVDs from a total of $20$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{20}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{20!}{3!\\cdot 17!}=1140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $12$) action movie DVDs is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{2}=\\frac{12!}{2!\\cdot 10!}=66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $1$ (out of $8$) cartoon DVD is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{8}{C}_{1} & =\\frac{8!}{1!\\cdot 7!}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{12}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{8}{C}_{1}=66\\cdot 8=528.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{528}{1140}≈0.46=46%.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
